course: dictionary
lesson: 13559
title: Not being able to do something
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word inability means not being able to do something. You can see its parts: in- means not, and ability means being able to do something. So inability is the lack of a skill, power or means to do a job.
explain: «Her inability to swim meant she stayed at the edge of the pool.» The clue is stayed at the edge. If she could not swim, she would stay where it was safe. So inability means she could not do it.
explain: Try a swap. Put «could not» in place of inability: «Her could not to swim meant...» That is clumsy, so use «her inability to swim» as a phrase: inability to + a verb, such as to swim, to read or to pay.
fact: Inability is a noun. It names a state: a lack of ability.
fact: «Inability» means not being able to do something, or a lack of skill, power or means.
fact: Use the phrase an inability to + verb, as in «an inability to read».
fact: «Ability» means being able to do something, so inability is its opposite.
fact: Inability is usually uncountable, so we write «inability» without an s in everyday use.
exercise: «The boy's inability to ride a bike worried him.» What does inability mean here? | Riding a bike very fast | A new bike he wants to buy | *Not being able to ride a bike | Being scared of the dark
exercise: Which word is ability with in- added at the front? | invite | *inability | island | interest
exercise: «Her inability to pay the bill made her feel worried.» What does the sentence show? | *She could not pay the bill | She paid the bill twice | She forgot where the bill was | She paid the bill with a card
test: Which phrase means he could not hear well? | an ability to hear | *an inability to hear | a loud noise | a quiet room
test: «Sam's inability to find his way home made him sad.» What does inability mean? | Feeling very happy | A map of the town | Running very fast | *Not being able to do something
exam: Which sentence shows an inability? | *«He could not open the jar, however hard he tried.» | «He can run faster than anyone in the school.» | «The sun is shining over the park.» | «She won first prize in the race.»
